{"study_accession":"PRJNA1033573","secondary_study_accession":"SRP469248","title":"Systems biology analysis reveals distinct molecular signatures promoting immune responsiveness to the BNT162b COVID-19 vaccine [bulk RNA-seq]","description":"Human immune responses to COVID-19 vaccines display a large heterogeneity of induced immunity and the underlying immune mechanisms for this remain largely unknown. Using a systems biology approach, we longitudinally profiled a unique cohort of female high and low responders to the BNT162b vaccine, who were known from previous COVID-19 vaccinations to develop maximum and minimum immune responses to the vaccine. We utilized high dimensional flow cytometry, bulk and single cell mRNA sequencing and 48-plex serum cytokine analyses. We revealed early, transient immunological and molecular signatures that distinguished high from low responders and correlated with B and T cell responses measured 14 days later. High responders featured a distinct transcriptional activity of interferon-driven genes and genes connected to enhanced antigen presentation. This was accompanied by a robust cytokine response related to Th1 differentiation. Both transcriptome and serum cytokine signatures were confirmed in two independent confirmatory cohorts. Collectively, our data contribute to a better understanding of the immunogenicity of mRNA-based COVID-19 vaccines, which might lead to the optimization of vaccine designs for individuals with poor vaccine responses. Overall design: With the submitted dataset, we compared the transcriptonal profile of vaccine COVID-19 vaccine high-responders (HR) and low-responders (LR) before the third (booster) COVID-19 vaccination with BNT162b (pre), and 24 hours (24h), 48 hours (48h), and 14 days afterwards (14d).","pubmed":"38160529","geo_series":"","n_samples":38,"study_labels":[{"level":"study","event_code":"VACCINATION","certainty":"confirmed","delta_days":14.0,"delta_bucket":"A_FAR","time_precision":"approx","stage":